A||||6||85ZainoGT||Not sure if I need to replace my headlight motor?||||5||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||08-27-2001||10:53 PM||||ok, so at first I was thinking I needed to replace the motor on the drivers side headlight.. but here is what happens..  ok, when the lights are down, the motor keeps turning, like nothing told it to stop.. it also wont go up, it goes down fine(jus doesnt stop trying to go down)  is it my motor I need to replace? thanks...||||reg||5
Z||000001||Brandon86SE||08-27-2001||11:41 PM||||what's up Zaino, couldn't find a junked firebird nearby?  Remember it only cost me $10 Canadian to fix mine.  I'd say for that cheap it's worth it just to replace.  Save yourself some headaches, I'm sure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Primaris||08-28-2001||12:39 AM||||I had the same problem which I just fixed tonight.  Go to <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html</A>  and buy the head light rebuild kit for your year.  I have an '88 so it was an easy fix.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000003||72vinman||08-28-2001||01:16 AM||||I rebuilt my passenger side headlight on Friday with the Kit from Rodney Dickman. I received the kit within a couple of days of ordering and it came with detailed instructions. It took me about two hours to rebuild it and the majority of that time was spent trying to clean out the old grease from the gears that had become so hard that there were no more teeth in the gear. They were all filled with the hard grease. It was easy to do. Now the only problem is that it works too well. It is extremley smooth as opposed to the other one.<P>------------------<BR>72vinman@home.com<BR>Stock Black 86GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/iannoy/sign.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||08-28-2001||01:27 AM||||From your description of the behavior, it sounds like you have the "old" (relay) headlight system (used on 1984-1986 Fieros).<BR>It also sounds like the HTS (high torque switch) isn't tripping.<P>So, you either rebuild the motor (which probably has a worn nylon gear), or replace it with a known good one.  I'm pretty sure that will solve your problem.  If you go to the Michigan Fiero Club's website, they have a pretty good article on rebuilding those motors.  Ordinarily costs almost no money, just takes time.    <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||David Bartlett||08-28-2001||04:42 AM||||Its the nylon gear. Take the motor/gear assembly apart, mark and flip the large gear to a good side, seal and replace the rivets with bolts. (The headlight only uses 1/3rd of the gear).  It took me about 30mins and about 30cents worth of screws. I did mine about a year ago and its still going strong.<P>Oh yea, remember to replace the old grease with some new sythetic stuff.<P>Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||85ZainoGT||08-28-2001||04:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by David Bartlett:<BR><B>Its the nylon gear. Take the motor/gear assembly apart, mark and flip the large gear to a good side, seal and replace the rivets with bolts. (The headlight only uses 1/3rd of the gear).  It took me about 30mins and about 30cents worth of screws. I did mine about a year ago and its still going strong.<P>Oh yea, remember to replace the old grease with some new sythetic stuff.<P>Good Luck.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>well since that is the cheapest, i think I will be trying that first, thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Brandon -- I looked for the firebirds, tons of them out there, jus none of them had the headlight motor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> lol... ill look again if all goes bad on me.. thanks for the help everyone..||||reg||1
